[section:jacobi Jacobi Polynomials]

[h4 Synopsis]

``
#include <boost/math/special_functions/jacobi.hpp>
``

   namespace boost{ namespace math{

   template<typename Real>
   Real jacobi(unsigned n, Real alpha, Real beta, Real x);

   template<typename Real>
   Real jacobi_derivative(unsigned n, Real alpha, Real beta, Real x, unsigned k);

   template<typename Real>
   Real jacobi_prime(unsigned n, Real alpha, Real beta, Real x);

   template<typename Real>
   Real jacobi_double_prime(unsigned n, Real alpha, Real beta, Real x);

   template <class T>
   std::vector<T> jacobi_zeros(unsigned n, T alpha, T beta);

   template <class T, class ``__Policy``>
   std::vector<T> jacobi_zeros(unsigned n, T alpha, T beta, const ``__Policy``&);

   }} // namespaces

Jacobi polynomials are a family of orthogonal polynomials.

A basic usage is as follows:

    using boost::math::jacobi;
    double x = 0.5;
    double alpha = 0.3;
    double beta = 7.2;
    unsigned n = 3;
    double y = jacobi(n, alpha, beta, x);

All derivatives of the Jacobi polynomials are available.
The /k/-th derivative of the /n/-th Gegenbauer polynomial is given by

    using boost::math::jacobi_derivative;
    double x = 0.5;
    double alpha = 0.3;
    double beta = 7.2;
    unsigned n = 3;
    double y = jacobi_derivative(n, alpha, beta, x, k);

For consistency with the rest of the library, `jacobi_prime` is provided which simply returns `jacobi_derivative(n, lambda, x,1)`.

[$../graphs/jacobi.svg]

`jacobi_zeros` returns the /n/ zeros of the Jacobi polynomial /P/[sub /n/][super ([alpha], [beta])], in increasing order;
they all lie in (-1, 1). For /n/ = 0 the result is empty.
[alpha] and [beta] must be finite and greater than -1, where the polynomials are orthogonal for the weight
(1 - /x/)[super [alpha]] (1 + /x/)[super [beta]] on \[-1, 1\];
otherwise the result is a vector of /n/ values returned by __domain_error.
These are the abscissas of the [link math_toolkit.gauss_jacobi Gauss-Jacobi quadrature] rule.

The zeros are found in O(/n/) operations by following the differential equation of the Jacobi functions
from zero to zero in twice the working precision (Glaser, Liu and Rokhlin), and near the end points from the series of the polynomials there.
Where that cannot vouch for a zero, the zeros come from the three-term recurrence instead, which overflows for large /n/ combined with
large [alpha] or [beta] (in `double`, for example /n/ = 20000 and [alpha] = [beta] = 200): the result is then a vector of values returned by __evaluation_error.
In our tests against high-precision values they are correctly rounded in `float`, `double` and 50-digit precision.

[optional_policy]

[h3 Implementation]

The implementation uses the 3-term recurrence for the Jacobi polynomials, rising.


[endsect]
